-- title:
--   Hilbert irreducibility: descent from $\mathbb{Q}(t)$ to $\mathbb{Q}$
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a finite group. If $G$ is the Galois group of some Galois extension of the rational function field $\mathbb{Q}(t)$, then $G$ is the Galois group of some Galois extension of $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   This is the specialization step supplied by Hilbert's irreducibility theorem: a Galois extension of $\mathbb{Q}(t)$ with group $G$ is generated by a root of an irreducible $f(t,X) \in \mathbb{Q}(t)[X]$, and for infinitely many rational values $t = a$ the specialized polynomial $f(a,X)$ stays irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}$ and its splitting field has Galois group $G$. The statement is a conditional one: it assumes realizability over $\mathbb{Q}(t)$ and concludes realizability over $\mathbb{Q}$.
-- source:
--   Inverse Galois problem, Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverse_Galois_problem (revision of 13 September 2026)

